    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let $t$ be the positive value of the imaginary part of the complex number $4+ti$.  \n$|4+ti| = 2\\sqrt{13}$.  \nBy applying the formula for the distance from a point to a line in a point-slope form,  \nwe get $|4| = 2\\sqrt{13} = \\sqrt{t^2 + 16}$.  \nSquaring both sides yields $16 = 13 = t^2 + 16$,  \nsolving which gives $t = \\boxed{1}$.  \n**Analysis:** This problem translates the given complex number into a form involving modulus in a specified absolute value $4+ti$ under the condition of the imaginary number $t$. By equating this modulus to the given absolute value, it converts the form of complex numbers to a distance relationship, making it easy to calculate.",
