    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the positive value of \\( t \\) for which \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we need to use the definition of the modulus (or absolute value) of a complex number. The modulus of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\). In this case, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\), so we can write:\n\n\\[ |{-4 + ti}| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2} \\]\n\nAccording to the problem, this modulus is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\). Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13} \\]\n\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2 \\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13 \\]\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = 52 \\]\n\nNext, we isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[ t^2 = 52 - 16 \\]\n\\[ t^2 = 36 \\]\n\nTaking the positive square root of both sides (since we are looking for the positive value of \\( t \\)), we get:\n\n\\[ t = \\sqrt{36} \\]\n\\[ t = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
